From 3a84c65fa05c1dff2266fefd829f4848eb93e623 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Filyus <filyus@filyus.ru>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 00:58:05 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat: add tokenHelper, a program that prints a registry
 token

`//<registry>/:tokenHelper=<absolute path>` names a program that prints the
credential for a registry, the setting of the same name that pnpm has. A raw
token is sent as `Bearer <token>`; output that starts with an auth scheme and
a space is used as the header value. The credential is supplied through the
`getAuthorization({ regKey, uri })` option of npm-registry-fetch, so it is sent
as a stored `_authToken` is: same proxy and certificate settings, same
redirects. npm-registry-fetch moves to ^20.1.0, the first version with the
option, and a command that makes requests fails with ETOKENHELPER when a helper
is configured and the library in use lacks it, instead of sending every request
without a credential.

The runner (lib/utils/token-helper.js, on @npmcli/promise-spawn) takes one
absolute path with no arguments and refuses anything else, and a value that is
not an existing file is never printed: the messages name the config key and say
the value is not shown. A .cmd or .bat file runs through cmd.exe, and a path
in a directory longer than 258 characters is refused. The helper starts in its own directory with stdin closed, has 60
seconds (SIGTERM, SIGKILL 2 seconds later, the process tree on Windows) and
1 MiB per stream, is read for 250 ms after it exits, and runs once per
registry and helper per process; a failure and a refusal are cached too. Its
environment lacks the npm_config_* variables and the script directories npm put
in front of PATH, and gets NODE_OPTIONS as the user's environment had it when
npm started (an npm that a script started recognises its parent's value by
npm_config_node_options). NPM_INTERNAL_TOKEN_HELPER_RUNNING is set for the
helper, and an npm it runs fails with ETOKENHELPER instead of running a helper.
Output is trimmed, a byte order mark removed, and anything that is not a header
value refused without quoting it. stderr is shown with the exact token replaced
by `***`: as warnings when the helper fails, at verbose when it works. A failure
is an ETOKENHELPER error whose message names the exit status and that carries
the signal, so npm exits as for any of its errors.

The credential does not go where a stored token would be sent but a user did
not choose to send it. A request over plain http to a host that is not this
machine is refused for every request (the registry key has no scheme). So is
any request while ca or cafile has its value from the project's configuration,
or strict-ssl is false there, and an http (loopback) request while proxy or
https-proxy has; over https a proxy sees only a CONNECT tunnel. The message
names the key and where it comes from, and the helper is not run. The project's
configuration is the project .npmrc; a user or global file that the location
rule below does not trust, so GitLab's NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG=$CI_PROJECT_DIR/.npmrc
is the project's layer for these settings as for the tokenHelper key; a value in
a child npm's environment for a key that its parent npm named as taken from its
project (a parent exports its project settings as npm_config_* values, which the
child reads as the user's, so it also lists the keys in
npm_config_token_helper_project_transport, and the child passes the list on; this
holds in whatever directory the child runs, which for the install that prepares a
git dependency is the clone, with a project of its own); and the publishConfig of package.json, which publish and unpublish register with
the config (a key set on the command line wins over publishConfig). One check per
request covers all four, so a publish whose credential is an OIDC token never
asks the helper and is not refused. A token, `_auth` or username and password
given on the command line or in the environment is used instead of the helper,
which is not run, with one warning per registry; one in a file loses to the
helper as before. getCredentialsByURI reports the same, so whoami and publish
agree with the request path.

A child npm also inherits the paths of the user and global files as npm_config_userconfig,
npm_config_globalconfig and npm_config_prefix, which its parent's project .npmrc may have
chosen, and finds no .npmrc of the parent's project (a clone of a git dependency has
another project or none) to tell it so. The parent therefore names the user and global
files it did not trust that hold a tokenHelper key in npm_config_token_helper_untrusted,
and a child does not trust them, whatever path its environment gives and wherever it
runs, and passes the list on. Nothing is exported unless a file holds a helper key.

The update check that any command may start once a week asks no helper: its request
for the public npm package carries no credential from one.

Only the user and global config files may set it. In project config,
publishConfig, the environment and on the command line it is ignored with a
warning, and so is the user file when project config sets userconfig, the global
file when it sets globalconfig or prefix (keys compared after ${VAR}
expansion), or a user or global file when its path was chosen on the command line or in the environment and lies inside a project.
Where the project is comes from the file alone: its real path and each
directory above it, from the file's own up to the filesystem root, stopping
without looking at the home directory; a directory with a package.json or a
.git (a file in a worktree or a submodule) is a project, node_modules is not a
mark. The current directory, --prefix, --global and location play no part, so a
project .npmrc cannot switch the check off, and a stray package.json in home
does not make ~/.npmrc.work or ~/.config/npm/npmrc a project's. The warning
names the directory and the mark, and the remedy. The unscoped, slash-less and
per-package-scope forms are ignored with a warning that names the line to write
instead.

The helper wins over a stored credential in a file for the same registry, with
one warning per registry that names what is ignored; an OIDC trusted-publishing
token wins over both, as the token it sets in the cli layer is a credential
given for the run. publish, whoami and get-identity count a helper as a
login. `npm login`
refuses for a registry with a helper, since the token it saved would never be
sent and its requests would carry the helper's credential, and `npm logout`
says that the setting has to be removed and names a token stored beside it
without revoking it, instead of revoking a token the helper's credential
replaces.
