 In order to scope these values, they must be prefixed by a URI fragment.
 If the credential is meant for any request to a registry on a single host, the scope may look like `//registry.npmjs.org/:`.
 If it must be scoped to a specific path on the host that path may also be provided, such as
 `//my-custom-registry.org/unique/path:`.
 
+#### Token helpers
+
+`tokenHelper` names a program that npm runs to get the credential for a registry, instead of reading a token stored in a file.
+It is pnpm's setting of the same name, so one helper serves both package managers from the same file:
+
+```ini
+//npm.corp.example.com/:tokenHelper=/usr/local/bin/corp-registry-token
+```
+
+The value is one absolute path with no arguments.
+It may contain spaces; unlike pnpm, npm does not split it into a command and arguments, and a bare name is refused so that a program in a project cannot be chosen through `PATH`.
+A command that needs arguments goes in a two-line script, and the setting names the script.
+For example, for AWS CodeArtifact, on Linux and macOS (`chmod +x` it):
+
+```sh
+#!/bin/sh
+exec aws codeartifact get-authorization-token --domain my-domain --domain-owner 111122223333 --query authorizationToken --output text
+```
+
+and on Windows, where a `.cmd` file is run through `cmd.exe` (a directory path longer than 258 characters is refused):
+
+```bat
+@echo off
+aws codeartifact get-authorization-token --domain my-domain --domain-owner 111122223333 --query authorizationToken --output text
+```
+
+The program prints the credential on one line.
+A raw token is sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, and output that starts with an auth scheme and a space, such as `Basic …`, is used as the header value as it is.
+Spaces around the output and a byte order mark are removed.
+A registry that has both a `tokenHelper` and a stored `_authToken` in a file uses the helper, and npm warns once, naming the credential it ignores.
+A credential given for the run itself, `--//host/:_authToken=…` or `npm_config_//host/:_authToken`, or the same for `_auth` or `username` and `_password`, is used instead: the helper is not run, and npm warns once for the registry, naming the key.
+It does not fall back to the stored token: a token left over from before the helper was set up has usually expired, and would answer 401 where the helper's would not.
+A token stored under a longer registry key than the helper's is the more specific credential and is used.
+While an OIDC trusted-publishing token is in use for a registry, it is sent instead of the helper's credential.
+`npm login` refuses for a registry with a helper, and `npm logout` says that the setting has to be removed.
+
+The helper is run the first time a request to that registry needs a credential, once per npm process, and its outcome is kept for the rest of the process: a failure is not retried either.
+Credentials are resolved before the cache is consulted, so the helper also runs for `--prefer-offline` and for a package the cache could have answered, and a failing helper fails every request to that registry, public packages included (a failure is visible, not a silent request without credentials).
+It is never run for an `--offline` request.
+Since it is not told which request it serves and cannot say how long its token is good for, a program that is slow or needs a login should keep what it fetched, in a cache of its own, so that starting it is cheap.
+
+The helper does not see the project.
+It starts in its own directory, with nothing on its standard input, and has 60 seconds and 1 MiB of output; npm ends it with SIGTERM and, 2 seconds later, SIGKILL (on Windows, the whole process tree), so a helper script should `exec` the command that does the work. Once the helper has exited npm reads its output for at most 250 ms more, so a background process it left running does not keep npm waiting.
+Its environment is npm's own without what npm set from a project's configuration: the `npm_config_*` variables, and `NODE_OPTIONS` where npm set it from `node-options`, which gets the value the user's environment had (an `npm` that a script started, finding it set by its parent, passes none on).
+When npm runs inside `npm run`, the `node_modules/.bin` directories and `node-gyp-bin` it added in front of `PATH` are removed, so the helper's tools are found where the user installed them, and a tool that only a project installs is called by its absolute path.
+An `npm` that the helper itself runs finds the variable `NPM_INTERNAL_TOKEN_HELPER_RUNNING` and fails with `ETOKENHELPER` where it would have run a helper, so a wrapper that calls `npm` cannot loop.
+Lines the helper writes to standard error are shown as warnings when it fails and at `--loglevel verbose` when it works, with the token it printed replaced by `***`.
+
+If a request fails with `ETOKENHELPER`, run it again with `--loglevel verbose` to see the helper's output.
+The message names the cause: a value that is not an absolute path or has arguments, a helper that is not found or cannot be started, a non-zero exit status or a signal, an empty token, output that is more than one line or not printable ASCII (the message does not quote it), more than 1 MiB of output, or more than 60 seconds.
+npm does not fall back to another credential for that registry.
+A value that is not an existing file is never printed, because it may be a token pasted by mistake: the messages name the config key and say the value is not shown.
+
+Because npm executes the program, `tokenHelper` is honored only from the per-user and global config files, and only when the repository did not choose them: a project `.npmrc` must not set `userconfig` (for the user file) or `globalconfig` or `prefix` (for the global file; settings are compared after `${VAR}` expansion), or the helper in that file is ignored with a warning.
+A user or global file whose path was chosen on the command line or in the environment (not left at its default) is ignored the same way when it lies inside a project, since the repository can have written it.
+Where the project is comes from the file alone: npm takes its real path, so that a link cannot hide it, and looks at the directory the file is in and each one above it, up to the filesystem root.
+A directory that holds a `package.json` or a `.git` (a file in a worktree or a submodule) is a project (`npm ci --prefix sub`, `--userconfig ./ci.npmrc`, `NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG=$CI_PROJECT_DIR/.npmrc`); `node_modules` is not a mark.
+The walk stops at the home directory without looking at it, so a file in `~` or below it, such as `~/.npmrc.work` or `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/npm/npmrc`, is the user's even when `~` has a `package.json`.
+The current directory, `--prefix`, `--global` and `location` play no part, so a project `.npmrc` cannot switch the check off.
+The warning names the directory that made the file a project, and what to do: use the default path (`~/.npmrc`) for the file, or move the `tokenHelper` line into the default user file.
+The rule has three limits.
+A `HOME` that is set to the checkout makes the default user file the repository's own `.npmrc`, which is not a chosen path; every tool trusts `HOME`, and git runs programs named in `~/.gitconfig`.
+Two paths of one directory that a real path does not join, such as a Linux bind mount, a Windows administrative share of a local drive, or a case-insensitive mount on Linux, are not recognised as the same.
+A file chosen inside a directory below home that is managed by git, such as `~/.config` with a `.git` for dotfiles, is refused, with the remedy.
+It is also ignored with a warning in a project `.npmrc`, in `publishConfig`, in `npm_config_*` environment variables and on the command line.
+Unlike pnpm, npm does not accept an unscoped `tokenHelper` for the default registry, since a project can change which registry is the default, nor a helper per package scope (`//host/:@scope:tokenHelper`) or a key without the slash before the colon (`//host:tokenHelper`); each is ignored with a warning that names the line to write instead.
+
+npm refuses to start a command that makes requests while a helper is configured and the `npm-registry-fetch` it runs is older than 20.1.0, which is the version that can ask a helper: it would send the requests with no credential.
+The credential is sent as a stored `_authToken` for the same registry is: with the same proxy and certificate settings and the same redirects.
+Two things a stored token would be sent over are refused for the helper's credential, with `ETOKENHELPER` and without running the helper.
+A request over plain `http:` to a host that is not this machine (`localhost`, `127.0.0.0/8`, `[::1]`) is refused: use `https:`.
+A request is also refused when `ca` or `cafile` has its value from the project's configuration, or `strict-ssl` is `false` there, since a repository could then route the credential through a server of its choosing; the message names the key and where it comes from.
+So it is when `proxy` or `https-proxy` does and the request is over plain `http:` (only a loopback one is allowed at all), which npm sends to the proxy itself; over `https:` a proxy only tunnels the connection, and the setting does not matter.
+The project's configuration is, for these settings as for `tokenHelper`: a project `.npmrc`; a user or global file that the rules above do not trust (one chosen inside a project, such as `NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG=$CI_PROJECT_DIR/.npmrc`, or named by the project's `.npmrc`); a value in the environment that a parent `npm` named as taken from its project (see below); and the `publishConfig` of `package.json` for `npm publish` and `npm unpublish`, which replaces the other layers for the request as it does in `npm publish`.
+The check is made for each request, so a publish whose credential is an OIDC token, which replaces the helper, is not refused.
+An `npm` that another `npm` started (a script that runs `npm publish`, or the install that prepares a git dependency in its own clone, which has another project or none) gets the project's settings as `npm_config_*` variables, so the parent also names the keys that came from the project in `npm_config_token_helper_project_transport` and the child treats a listed key with a value in its environment as the project's, whatever directory it runs in, and names it for its own children. The child compares nothing with a `.npmrc` of its own directory. An environment value that the parent did not list is the user's own, even when a project's `.npmrc` holds the same value.
+The same child also inherits the paths of the user and global files as `npm_config_userconfig`, `npm_config_globalconfig` and `npm_config_prefix`, which a project's `.npmrc` may have chosen in the parent, so the parent names the files it did not trust and that hold a `tokenHelper` key in `npm_config_token_helper_untrusted`, and the child does not trust them whatever the path in its environment says and whatever directory it runs in.
+The weekly check for a newer npm, which any command may start, sends no credential from a helper.
+Remove the setting from the project's `.npmrc` or `publishConfig`, or set it on the command line, which overrides project config.
+
 ### Unsupported Custom Configuration Keys
 
 Starting in npm v11.2.0, npm warns when unknown configuration keys are defined in `.npmrc`. In a future major version of npm, these unknown keys may no longer be accepted.
