-The message names the cause: a value that is not an absolute path or has arguments, a helper that is not found or cannot be started, a non-zero exit status or a signal, an empty token, output that is more than one line or not printable ASCII (the message does not quote it), more than 1 MiB of output, or more than 60 seconds.
+The message names the cause: a value that is not an absolute path or has arguments, a helper that is not found or cannot be started (for a script, its `#!` interpreter is named), a non-zero exit status or a signal, an empty token, output that is more than one line or not printable ASCII (the message does not quote it), more than 1 MiB of output, or more than 60 seconds.
+Exit status 127 means that a tool the script calls was not found: give its absolute path.
 npm does not fall back to another credential for that registry.
 A value that is not an existing file is never printed, because it may be a token pasted by mistake: the messages name the config key and say the value is not shown.
+A request that the registry answers with 401 while a helper is configured says that the helper's token was refused, and to refresh the login or the cache of the tool behind it, not to run `npm login`.
 
 Because npm executes the program, `tokenHelper` is honored only from the per-user and global config files, and only when the repository did not choose them: a project `.npmrc` must not set `userconfig` (for the user file) or `globalconfig` or `prefix` (for the global file; settings are compared after `${VAR}` expansion), or the helper in that file is ignored with a warning.
 A user or global file whose path was chosen on the command line or in the environment (not left at its default) is ignored the same way when it lies inside a project, since the repository can have written it.
